¶
'''threap, threep''' (thrēp), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''threpe''; < ME. ''threpen'', ''thræpen'', < AS. ''threápian'', reprove, rebuke, afflict.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To contradict.

{{smaller block|Thou wilt not ''threap'' me, this whinyard has gard many better men to lope than thou.
{{right|''Greene'', James IV., Int.}}}}

'''2.''' To aver or affirm with pertinacious repetition; continue to assert with contrary obstinacy, as in reply to persistent denial: as, to ''threap'' a thing down one's throat.

{{smaller block|Behold how gross a Ly of Ugliness
They on my face have ''threaped.''
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, v. 227.}}}}

'''3.''' To insist on.

{{smaller block|He ''threappit'' to see the auld hardened blood-shedder.
{{right|''Scott'', St. Ronan's Well, xiv.}}}}

'''4.''' To cry out; complain; contend; maintain.

{{smaller block|Some crye upon God, some other ''threpe'' that he hathe forgoten theym.
{{right|''Bp. Fisher'', Sermons. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''5.''' To call; term.

{{smaller block|Sol gold is, and Luna silver we ''threpe.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Canon's Yeoman's Tale, l. 273.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To indulge in mutual recrimination or contradiction; contend; quarrel; bandy words; dispute.

{{smaller block|Thei thaste hym full thraly, than was ther no ''threpyng'',
Thus with dole was that dere vn-to dede dight,
His bak and his body was bolned for betyng,
Itt was, I saie the for soth, a sorowfull sight.
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 430.}}}}

{{smaller block|It 's not for a man with a woman to ''threepe.''
{{right|''Take Thine old Cloak about Thee.''}}}}

