2. To fight; battle.

{{smaller block|Than thretty dayes throly thei ''thrappit'' in feld,<br>And mony bold in the bekur were on bent leuit!
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 8362.}}}}

[Obsolete or prov. Eng. or Scotch in all uses.]

'''threap, threep''' (thrēp), ''n.'' [< ME. ''threpe, threp''; < ''threap, v.''] 1†. Contest; attack.

{{smaller block|What! thinke ye so throly this ''threpe'' for to leue?<br>Heyue vp your herttes, henttes your armys;<br>Wackyns vp your willes, as worthy men shuld.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 9850.}}}}

2†. Contradiction.—3. A vehement or pertinacious affirmation; an obstinate decision or determination. [Prov. Eng. and Scotch.]

{{smaller block|You would show more patience, and perhaps more prudence, if you sought not to overwork me by shrewd words and sharp ''threaps'' of Scripture.
{{right|''T. Cromwell'', quoted in R. W. Dixon's Hist. Church [of Eng., vii.}}}}

{{smaller block|He has taken a ''threap'' that he would have it finished before the year was done.
{{right|''Carlyle.''}}}}

4. A superstitious idea or notion; a freet.

{{smaller block|They'll . . . hae an auld wife when they're dying to rhyme ower prayers, and ballants, and charms, . . . rather than they'll hae a minister to come and pray wi' them—that 's an auld ''threep'' o' theirs. {{right|''Scott'', Guy Mannering, xlv.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To keep one's threap''', to stick pertinaciously or obstinately to one 's averments or assertions. ''Scott'', Bride of Lammermoor, xxvii.}}

'''threasure†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''treasure''. ''Spenser.''

'''threat''' (thret), ''n.'' [< ME. ''thret, threte, thræt, thrat, threat'', < AS. ''threát'', a crowd, troop, pressure, trouble, calamity, threat (= Icel. ''thraut'', trouble, labor), < ''threótan'' (pret. ''threát'', pp. ''throten''), urge, afflict, vex, in comp. ''ā-threótan'', im-

