pers., vex, = D. ''ver-drieten'', vex, = OHG. *''driozan'', in comp. ''bi-driozan'' (MHG. ''bedriezen''), ''irdriozan'' (MHG. ''er-driezen''), MHG. ''ver-driezen'', G. ''ver-driessen'', impers., vex, annoy, = Icel. ''thrjōta'', impers., fail, = Dan. ''fortryde'', vex, repent, = Goth. *''thriutan'', in ''us-thriutan'' (= AS. ''ā-threótan''), trouble, vex, = L. ''trudere'', push, shove, crowd, thrust out, press, urge (> ''trudis'', a pole to push with), = OBulg. ''truzda'', vex, plague (''trudŭ'', trouble). From the same verb or its compounds are the nouns Icel. ''throt'', want, MHG. ''urdruz'', ''urdrütze'', vexation, ''verdruz'', G. ''verdruss'' (= Dan. ''fortræd''), vexation, trouble. Hence ''threat'', ''v.'', ''threaten''. Cf. ''thrust''<sup>1</sup>. From the L. verb are ult. E. ''extrude'', ''intrude'', ''protrude'', etc., ''trusion'', ''extrusion'', etc.] '''1†.''' Crowd; press; pressure.

{{smaller block|The ''thræt'' was the mare.{{right|''Layamon'', l. 9791.}}}}

'''2†.''' Vexation; torment.

{{smaller block|Then ''thrat'' moste I thole, & vnthonk to mede.{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), iii. 55.}}}}

'''3.''' A menace; a denunciation of ill to befall some one; a declaration of an intention or a determination to inflict punishment, loss, or pain on another.

{{smaller block|There is no terror, Cassius, in your ''threats''.{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iv. 3. 66.}}}}

{{smaller block|'Tis certain that the ''threat'' is sometimes more formidable than the stroke, and 'tis possible that the beholders suffer more keenly than the victims. ''Emerson'', Courage.}}

readings.c2s2:
4. In ''law'', any menace of such a nature and extent as to unsettle the mind of the person on whom it operates, and to preclude that free voluntary action which is necessary to assent.

{{smaller|=Syn. 3. See ''menace, v. t.''}}

¶'''threat''' (thret), ''v.'' [< ME. ''threten'', < AS. ''threátian'', press, oppress, repress, correct, threaten (= MD. ''droten'', threaten), < ''threát'', pressure: see ''threat, n.'' Cf. ''threaten''.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To press; urge; compel.

{{smaller block|Fele thryuande thonkkeȝ he ''thrat'' hom to haue.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1980.}}}}

2. To threaten.

{{smaller block|Every day this wal they wolde ''threte''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 754.}}}}

II. ''intrans.'' To use threats; act or speak menacingly; threaten.

{{smaller block|''K. Phi.'' Look to thyself, thou art in jeopardy.
''K. John.'' No more than he that ''threats''.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iii. 1. 347.}}}}

{{smaller block|'Twere wrong with Rome, when Catiline and thou
Do ''threat'', if Cato feared. ''B. Jonson'', Catiline, iii. 1.}}

[Obsolete or archaic in all senses.]

'''threaten''' (thret′n), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thretnen''; < ''threat'' + ''-en''<sup>1</sup>.] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To use threats or menaces; have a menacing aspect.

{{smaller block|An eye like Mars, to ''threaten'' and command.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 4. 57.}}}}

'''2.''' To give indication of menace, or of impending danger or mischief; become overcast, as the sky.

{{smaller block|I have long waited to answer your kind letter of August 20th, in hopes of having something satisfactory to write to you; but I have waited in vain, for every day our political horizon blackens and ''threatens'' more and more.
{{right|''T. A. Mann'' (Ellis's Lit. Letters, p. 437).}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To declare an intention of doing mischief to or of bringing evil on; use threats toward; menace; terrify, or attempt to terrify, by menaces: with ''with'' before the evil threatened.

{{smaller block|This letter he early bid me give his father,
And ''threaten'd'' me ''with'' death, going in the vault,
If I departed not and left him there.
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., v. 3. 276.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Threaten'' your enemies,
And prove a valiant tongue-man.
{{right|''Ford'', Lady's Trial, iii. 3.}}}}

'''2.''' To charge or enjoin solemnly or with menace.

{{smaller block|Let us straitly ''threaten'' them, that they speak henceforth to no man in this name.
{{right|Acts iv. 17.}}}}

'''3.''' To be a menace or source of danger to.

{{smaller block|He ''threatens'' many that hath injured one.
{{right|''B. Jonson.''}}}}

'''4.''' To give ominous indication of; presage; portend: as, the clouds ''threaten'' rain or a storm.

{{smaller block|Batteries on batteries guard each fatal pass,
''Threatening'' destruction.
{{right|''Addison'', The Campaign.}}}}

{{smaller block|The feeling of the blow of a stick or the sight of a ''threatened'' blow will change the course of action which a dog would otherwise have pursued.
{{right|''Mivart'', Nature and Thought, p. 210.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|He [a janizary] ''threatened'' to detain us, but at last permitted us to go on, and we staid that night at a large convent near. {{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. i. 251.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Threatening'' torments unendurable,
If any harm through treachery befell.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 152.}}}}

{{smaller|=Syn. 4. ''Menace, Threaten'' (see ''menace''), forebode, foreshadow.}}

'''threatener''' (thret′nėr), ''n.'' [< ''threaten'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who threatens; one who indulges in threats or menaces.

{{smaller block|Threaten the ''threatener'', and outface the brow
Of bragging horror. {{right|''Shak.'', K. John, v. 1. 49.}}}}

'''threatening''' (thret′ning), ''n.'' [< ME. ''thretninge''; verbal n. of ''threaten'', ''v.''] The act of one who threatens; a threat; a menace; a menacing.

{{smaller block|They constrain him not with ''threatenings'' to dissemble his mind, and shew countenance contrary to his thought. {{right|''Sir T. More'', Utopia (tr. by Robinson), ii. 11.}}}}

'''threatening''' (thret′ning), ''p. a.'' 1. Indicating or containing a threat or menace.

{{smaller block|The ''threatening'' alliance between Science and the Revolution is not really directed in favor of atheism nor against theology. {{right|''J. R. Seeley'', Nat. Religion, p. 41.}}}}

